{"id":"418035b4-92e0-4ef2-bca5-0f3db151b94b","type":"level","createdDate":"2026-06-13T23:10:16.026Z","createdBy":"chaitanya.ponnapati@codeninjas.com","lastModifiedDate":"2026-06-13T23:10:16.026Z","lastModifiedBy":"chaitanya.ponnapati@codeninjas.com","attributes":{"tags":["CREATE"],"title":"Level 1","sequence":1,"activity":{"id":"54a33050-5370-46d1-bb82-44a00611c914","type":"activity","sequence":2,"group":{"id":"54a33050-5370-46d1-bb82-44a00611c914","type":"build","name":null,"sequence":5,"subgroups":[{"id":"54a33050-5370-46d1-bb82-44a00611c914","type":"subgroup","sequence":1,"name":"build 1","steps":[{"id":"7331d692-4fef-4bce-b233-b1c365753093","hints":"<p>❓ Did you type a number between 1-15 to set the color parameter inside the parentheses in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">scene.setBackgroundColor(color)</strong>?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" height=\"27.792125474541468\" width=\"344\"></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/rvfkk5w2.wji.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ How do you change the color of the background?&nbsp;</p><p>❓ How can you test out the background color code to see what color appears on the screen?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that in Green Belt, they used the numerical color values to change the color in a background image using a <strong>[change color in (picture) from () to ()] </strong>block. Each color is assigned to a number based on its order in the color palette. Encourage the ninja to use different numbers as <strong>arguments </strong>and play the project to see what color appears. They can also use the color chart included in the instructions to help them select a color to use.</p><p>💡 Even though there are only 16 colors listed in the color palette, the ninja is technically able to input a value outside of the 1-15 range. The background color will still appear on the screen and the project will be able to run.</p>","instructions":"<p>Add a background color to the project!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Scene,</strong> drag out <strong>[set background color to (color)] </strong>into the editor<strong>. </strong>Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><p><br></p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">scene.setBackgroundColor(0)\n</pre><p>Set a color parameter using a number. Click between the parentheses and delete <span class=\"ql-font-monospace\">0</span>. In the code editor, there are 15 colors available. Type a number between 1-15 to try out different background colors.</p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/mbvbcllr.vy5.png\"></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the background color appear on the screen.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"b9618800-46d2-4c6b-a893-f183a9ad3cea","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">❓ Did you add a sprite, select its image, and update its name? </span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/knlmwwaf.ijr.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ What should you click to change the sprite image?</p><p>❓ What might be a good name for the sprite image chosen?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Guide the ninja in dragging the code block into the JavaScript editor, so that it appears on the correct line. They can drag the block to the end of the first block of code, so that it appears directly below it.</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that they can minimize the code for the sprite image by clicking the arrow to the right of the line number.</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that the sprite name should be written in camelCase, using capitalized letters to indicate where spaces would be.</p>","instructions":"<p>Add a sprite<strong> </strong>to this project!&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites</strong>, drag out <strong>[sprite (img) of kind (kind)]</strong> onto a blank line under the existing code. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">let mySprite = sprites.create(img`...`, SpriteKind.Player)\n</pre><p>Click the palette <img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" height=\"26\" width=\"27\">to the left of the code to select a sprite. In the editor, highlight <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>and replace it by typing a new name for the sprite. Give the sprite a simple name that describes what it is, such as <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">square</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the sprite appear on the screen.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"1258da3c-c698-43d3-b6b6-44b9fb049047","hints":"<p>❓ Did you try different vx and vy values in the <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setVelocity(vx, vy)</strong> code?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/q104wixj.uj5.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Which <strong>argument </strong>changes the sprite’s left and right movement? Which <strong>argument </strong>changes the sprite’s up and down movement?&nbsp;</p><p>❓ Why is it important to rename the sprite in each line of code? How is this similar to coding in blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡The first <strong>argument </strong>in the<strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setVelocity(vx,vy</strong><strong>) </strong>code sets the vx value, which changes the speed and direction (left or right) that the sprite moves in. The second <strong>argument </strong>sets the vy value, which changes the speed and direction (up or down) that the sprite moves in. Encourage the ninja to test out different values to see how it affects the sprite’s movement.</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that in blocks, they would select the correct sprite name from the drop down menu in each code block. The same happens in text, but instead, they simply replace the default <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>by typing in the correct sprite name.</p>","instructions":"<p>Make the sprite move on its own!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites, </strong>drag out <strong>[set (mySprite) velocity to vx vy]</strong> onto a blank line under the existing code. <strong>&nbsp;</strong>Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:&nbsp;</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">mySprite.setVelocity(50, 50)\n</pre><p>Rename <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite </strong>to the sprite name defined in step 2. Replace the default <strong>arguments </strong>of the<strong> </strong><strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setVelocity(50, 50)</strong> code and notice how it changes the sprite’s movement in different directions.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the sprite move in different directions.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"c6f9cdd1-890f-44ef-9a8d-bf0cc648548f","hints":"<p>❓Did you replace mySprite with the sprite’s name in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setBounceOnWall(on)</strong>?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/nkkm4fsw.2qg.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Why is the default <strong>argument </strong>in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setBounceOnWall(on)</strong> set to <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">true</strong>? What did this look like in blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡<strong>Arguments</strong> can also be Booleans. Remind the ninja that <strong>arguments </strong>are the value inside the parentheses (parameter) that pass information to the function.</p><p>💡Explain to the ninjas that just like in blocks, each code block has a different <strong>argument </strong>as its default. In the <strong>[set sprite bounce on walls]</strong> block, there is a toggle that can be used to set the effect &lt;ON&gt; or &lt;OFF&gt;. This appears as a Boolean in text, where <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">true</strong><strong> </strong>means the effect is turned on and <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">false</strong><strong> </strong>means the effect is turned off.</p>","instructions":"<p>Oh no! The sprite disappears when it moves off screen. Code the sprite to bounce on the walls of the screen.</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites, </strong>drag out <strong>[set (mySprite) bounce on wall (true)] </strong>onto a blank line under the existing code<strong>.&nbsp;</strong>Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">mySprite.setBounceOnWall(true)\n</pre><p>Rename <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>to the sprite name defined in step 2. Since <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">true</strong><strong> </strong>is the default <strong>argument</strong>, the <strong>[bounce on walls]</strong> effect will be turned <em>on</em> when the project begins.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the sprite bounce whenever it hits a wall!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"edc091da-130c-4cf8-b726-3c54870378f7","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">❓Did you switch back to the blocks editor? What looks similar and what looks different?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/jc52pz3j.v1j.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Does the code work as expected in blocks?</p><p>❓ When coding in text, what things do you need to be more mindful of, than when coding in blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Help the ninja make connections between the JavaScript text they have just written and their knowledge of blocks. Ninjas should be very familiar with these concepts already, so encourage them to think about the similarities and differences between blocks and text.</p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">💡 Encourage the Ninja to tinker with the values in the </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">[set sprite velocity] </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">block to access the visual supports in blocks. Prompt the Ninja to notice how the sprite’s movement changes and how the arguments passed in JavaScript reflect this.</span></p>","instructions":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Test out the project. Does it work as expected?</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Look over the </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">syntax </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">of the code in this project: the capitalization of certain letters, the spacing, and the symbols - commas, parentheses, and quotation marks. Notice that changing the syntax might make the project unable to run.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Switch to the blocks editor to see what the JavaScript code looks like in blocks! Notice what is similar about the code, and what looks different: how are sprite names and other parameters formatted in blocks, compared to JavaScript?</span></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" height=\"53\" width=\"294\"></span></p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null}],"title":"Bouncing on the Walls","project":{"editor":{"filters":[{"name":"controller","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"game_control_sprite","active":true},{"name":"keyonevent","active":true},{"name":"keyispressed","active":true}]},{"name":"music","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"music_string_playable","active":true},{"name":"music_playable_play","active":true},{"name":"music_stop_all_sounds","active":true},{"name":"synth_set_volume","active":true},{"name":"device_change_tempo","active":true},{"name":"device_set_tempo","active":true}]},{"name":"scene","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"scenescreenwidth","active":true},{"name":"scenescreenheight","active":true},{"name":"gamesetbackgroundcolor","active":true},{"name":"gamesetbackgroundimage","active":true},{"name":"particlesStartScreenAnimation","active":true}]},{"name":"loops","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"forever","active":true},{"name":"device_pause","active":true},{"name":"pxt_controls_for","active":true},{"name":"pxt_controls_for_of","active":true},{"name":"pxt-on-start","active":true}]},{"name":"logic","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"controls_if","active":true},{"name":"logic_operation","active":true},{"name":"logic_negate","active":true},{"name":"logic_boolean","active":true}]},{"name":"variables","active":false,"blocks":[]},{"name":"Math","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"math_js_round","active":true},{"name":"device_random","active":true},{"name":"percentchance","active":true}]},{"name":"images","active":false,"blocks":[]},{"name":"arrays","active":false,"blocks":[]},{"name":"text","active":false,"blocks":[]},{"name":"console","active":false,"blocks":[]},{"name":"light","active":false,"blocks":[]},{"name":"encoders","active":false,"blocks":[]}],"searchBar":true},"source":{"text":{"main.ts":"\n","pxt.json":"{\n    \"name\": \"Untitled\